Create these issues: Issue 1: Critical - WASM Loading Performance Issue
Title: Critical: WASM loading performance issue - base64 encoding on every cold start

Labels: bug, priority:critical

Body:

## Problem

The current implementation in `src/lib/calculation/ephemeris.ts` uses filesystem reads and base64 encoding for WASM loading, which creates a significant performance bottleneck:

- Loads entire 571KB WASM file into memory on EVERY cold start
- Base64 encodes it (resulting in 764KB) 
- Adds ~50-100ms latency on every cold start
- Wasteful - WASM is already being served as a static asset but not being used

## Current Implementation (lines 22-35)

The code reads from filesystem and converts to base64 data URL:
- File: `src/lib/calculation/ephemeris.ts:22-35`
- Method: filesystem read → base64 encode → data URL

## Recommended Solution

Use HTTP URLs to load WASM from the public directory, which:
- Leverages CDN caching
- Reduces memory usage
- Eliminates base64 encoding overhead
- Is the approach documented in the spec

## Impact

- **Performance**: ~50-100ms improvement per cold start
- **Memory**: ~764KB reduction per serverless instance
- **Scalability**: Better resource utilization at scale

## Related

- PR #15
- Documentation: `app_docs/bug-wasm-fetch-failed-vercel-production.md`
Issue 2: Critical - Race Condition in Singleton Initialization
Title: Critical: Race condition in WASM singleton initialization

Labels: bug, priority:critical

Body:

## Problem

The `getSwe()` function in `src/lib/calculation/ephemeris.ts` has a race condition when handling concurrent requests:

- If two concurrent requests call `calculateActivations()`, both may initialize WASM simultaneously
- The singleton pattern uses a simple null check without promise coordination
- This can lead to multiple WASM initializations, wasting resources and potentially causing issues

## Current Implementation (lines 22-35)

```typescript
async function getSwe(): Promise<SwissEPH> {
  if (!sweInstance) {  // ← Race condition here
    // ... initialation code
    sweInstance = await SwissEPH.init(wasmDataUrl);
  }
  return sweInstance;
}
Issue
When two requests arrive simultaneously:

Both check !sweInstance and see null
Both proceed to initialize WASM
Both call SwissEPH.init()
Resource waste and potential conflicts
Recommended Solution
Use a promise-based singleton pattern:

let sweInstance: SwissEPH | null = null;
let initPromise: Promise<SwissEPH> | null = null;

async function getSwe(): Promise<SwissEPH> {
  if (sweInstance) return sweInstance;

  if (!initPromise) {
    initPromise = (async () => {
      // ... WASM loading code
      sweInstance = await SwissEPH.init(wasmUrl);
      return sweInstance;
    })();
  }

  return initPromise;
}
Impact
Correctness: Ensures single initialization even with concurrent requests
Performance: Prevents wasteful duplicate initializations
Reliability: Reduces potential for initialization conflicts
Related
PR fix: WASM loading for Vercel production + Cosmic UI redesign #15
